// The gauntlet's first program, a dot product: ONE SOURCE for Go, JavaScript
// and Java, in the language's numbers (ADR 0086, numbers.md §9 part 4).
//
// Each target had its own copy, because the arithmetic was the host's: `go.f*`
// and `go.f+`, `js.*` over `any`, `java.f+`. A double is now an element of F64
// ⊂ N, and IEEE's arithmetic is the scope round(F64) { … }, which rounds each
// operation of N inside it to F64. So the sum is
//
//   s ← round(F64, s + round(F64, a[i] · b[i]))
//
// as the hand-written loop computes it, and each rounding is the target's own
// operation, found by its declaration's `ensure` (targets/*/; ir/doubles.go):
// Go's product is written `float64(x * y)`, which stops Go fusing it into the
// sum, the one thing the host would otherwise do differently.
//
// The delayed vector is the same three lines it always was: a library written
// in the language, not a primitive, which reduces away. Whether the fusion
// survives is what this program has always asked. `mul` rounds the product
// where it is made, because a scope is lexical and zip's λ is outside it.
//
// THE ENTRIES' SET IS WHAT MAKES THE SUM FINITE. Each product is within 10²⁰⁰,
// and a rounded accumulator moves by at most twice its increment per step
// (doubles.go, Theorem A), so after at most 2⁶³ steps the sum is within
// 2⁶⁴·10²⁰⁰, inside F64: every rounding's overflow obligation is proven, and
// nothing is checked at run time. The equal lengths make b[i] defined.
set Entry = set { x ∈ F64 : -1e100 <= x && x <= 1e100 }

fn vec(n, f) { fn(sel) { sel(n, f) } }
fn vlen(v) { v(fn(n, f) { n }) }
fn vindex(v, i) { v(fn(n, f) { f })(i) }
fn of_array(a) { vec(len(a), fn(i) { a[i] }) }

fn zip(g, a, b) { vec(vlen(a), fn(i) { g(vindex(a, i), vindex(b, i)) }) }
fn mul(x, y) { round(F64, x * y) }

fn sum(v) {
    n = vlen(v)
    round(F64) {
        loop acc = 0, i = 0 {
            i >= n -> acc
            else -> again(acc + vindex(v, i), i + 1)
        }
    }
}

export fn dot(a ∈ Array(Entry), b ∈ Array(Entry)) (F64) where len(a) == len(b) {
    sum(zip(mul, of_array(a), of_array(b)))
}
